### TL;DR

The idea is stupid; you're right.

### How lazy.nvim merges specs

Yes, [lazy.nvim](https://github.com/folke/lazy.nvim) merges ALL specs into one, as explained [here](https://lazy.folke.io/spec#spec-setup).

- If `config` is **not** defined, `lazy.nvim` tries to guess the module name and runs its setup automatically:
  ```lua
  require('<name>').setup(<merged-spec>)
  ```

- If `config` is defined, `lazy.nvim` uses the last declared `config` function and passes the merged spec as the second parameter:
  ```lua
  {
    config = function(_, opts)
      -- Custom configuration logic
    end
  }
  ```

By default, specs are deeply merged.
However, lists are replaced by the last processed value unless explicitly extended using [`opts_extend`](https://github.com/folke/lazy.nvim/discussions/1706#discussioncomment-10268907):

```lua
{
  opts_extend = { 'sources.name', ... },
  opts = {
    sources = {
      name = { 'list', 'that', 'will', 'be', 'extended' }
    }
  }
}
```

If a module is listed as a [dependency](https://lazy.folke.io/spec#spec-loading), it loads automatically when the main module is loaded—unless it’s already loaded:

> [!NOTE]
> The specs will be merged before the plugin is loaded, regardless of where they are defined.
> Merging specs is similar to preprocessing.

```lua
{
  'romgrk/barbar.nvim',
  event = { 'FileType', 'VimEnter' },
  dependencies = {
    { 'echasnovski/mini.icons', opts = { mock_nvim_web_devicons = true } }
  }
}
```

To prevent this, we can use the [`specs`](https://lazy.folke.io/spec#spec-advanced) option. This way, the specs are still merged, but the module remains optional and loads only if defined elsewhere:

```lua
{
  'romgrk/barbar.nvim',
  event = { 'FileType', 'VimEnter' },
  specs = {
    { 'echasnovski/mini.icons', opts = { mock_nvim_web_devicons = true } }
  }
}
```

### Why this might be a bad idea

- If no `config` is defined, but `mock_nvim_web_devicons = true` is merged from somewhere, all icons will be mocked.
  This can make debugging difficult.

- If `config` is defined and `mock_nvim_web_devicons` is overridden, why include this feature in the first place?
